  "description": "Simulate pandas reset_index by preserving or dropping custom index columns from structured row records.\n\nResetting the index converts hierarchical or filtered index labels into standard data columns, establishing default contiguous 0-indexed RangeIndex ordering.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{df.reset\\_index}(\\text{drop}=\\text{drop})\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Iterate over row records in dataframe dictionary format\n2. If drop is True, eliminate the specified index identifier field\n3. Return list of standardized dictionaries\n\n### Returns\nList of row dictionaries reflecting index manipulation.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Resetting Index\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Resetting Index** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nResetting the index converts hierarchical or filtered index labels into standard data columns, establishing default contiguous 0-indexed RangeIndex ordering. In this problem, the goal is to: simulate pandas reset_index by preserving or dropping custom index columns from structured row records.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{df.reset\\_index}(\\text{drop}=\\text{drop})\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Iterate over row records in dataframe dictionary format\n2. **Phase 2**: If drop is True, eliminate the specified index identifier field\n3. **Phase 3**: Return list of standardized dictionaries\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: df_data = [{\"user_id\": 101, \"val\": \"A\"}, {\"user_id\": 102, \"val\": \"B\"}], index_col = \"user_id\", drop = true\n\nOutput: [{\"val\": \"A\"}, {\"val\": \"B\"}]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_resetting_index under standard test conditions."
